# Sprint E3.4 cron heartbeat evidence (sanitized)
# run_id: 20261110-1701 (UTC)
# Production/dev PG 5432 and container 55432 were not touched.

## 1. Success rounds (cron against an isolated PostgreSQL 18.3 harness with --legacy V1 schema)
$ CRON_HEARTBEAT_FILE=<temp>/e34-heartbeat.json \
  node scripts/with-v2-test-db.mjs --legacy -- node node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs scripts/cron-inventory-daemon.ts
# task rounds succeeded (inventory-sync + order-expiry); the worker then kept polling and was
# stopped by the operator. Heartbeat file written by the real daemon code path:
{"updatedAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.471Z","tasks":{"inventory-sync":{"lastSuccessAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.460Z","consecutiveFailures":0,"lastRunAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.460Z","lastOutcome":"success"},"order-expiry":{"lastSuccessAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.471Z","consecutiveFailures":0,"lastRunAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.471Z","lastOutcome":"success"}}}

## 2. Controlled failure rounds (same heartbeat file; DATABASE_URL -> closed loopback port)
$ CRON_HEARTBEAT_FILE=<temp>/e34-heartbeat.json \
  DATABASE_URL=postgres://<placeholder>@127.0.0.1:<closed-port>/... \
  npx tsx scripts/cron-inventory-daemon.ts
# both rounds failed with Prisma P1001; heartbeat keeps the previous lastSuccessAt and
# increments consecutiveFailures (the daemon restores the file at startup):
{"updatedAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:14.872Z","tasks":{"inventory-sync":{"lastSuccessAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.460Z","consecutiveFailures":1,"lastRunAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:14.855Z","lastOutcome":"failure"},"order-expiry":{"lastSuccessAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:00.471Z","consecutiveFailures":1,"lastRunAt":"2026-11-10T17:01:14.872Z","lastOutcome":"failure"}}}
# The worker stayed alive after both failures (stopped only by the operator), so the polling
# and exit semantics are unchanged.

## 3. Staging cron healthcheck semantics (same node -e command as the compose healthcheck)
# Fresh heartbeat (last success < 30 min):  HEALTHCHECK_FRESH_EXIT=0
# Stale copy (lastSuccessAt moved 31 min back): HEALTHCHECK_STALE_EXIT=1
# => cron health is now based on "recent successful round", not on the web /api/health probe.

## 4. Notes
# - CRON_HEARTBEAT_FILE defaults to /tmp/flower-cron-heartbeat.json; staging compose sets
#   /var/run/flower/cron-heartbeat.json on both services and shares the `cron_heartbeat` volume.
# - Heartbeat writes are best effort: a filesystem error is swallowed and never changes the
#   round outcome or the worker loop.
# - The file contains only ISO timestamps, counters and outcome strings (no credentials,
#   no request data, no error text).
